#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "8ed57b1f-0082-5281-b061-ff2020ed7d66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12364" :question/problem "The fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=e^{x/2}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in [0,2]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big\\langle \\text{_______}\\,, \\:-e^{x/2} \\cos\\theta, \\: -e^{x/2}\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWhat is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=e^{x/2} \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=e^{x/2},$ and \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=e^{x/2},$ we obtain $f' = \\dfrac12 e^{x/2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st component of the product is $\\dfrac{1}{2}e^x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "a2ff0c76-0d26-51a2-a2fb-498ff5f85559" :question/math-academy-id "e-12365" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to find an integral expression for the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\sin z$ is rotated by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis for $z \\in [0, \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\sin z \\geq 0$ for all $z \\in [0, \\pi].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\sin z,$ and\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (z,\\theta) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi , \\, 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\sin z,$ we obtain $f'(z) = \\cos z.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "1d32df15-74ee-5a3d-bc33-3d6452700e84" :question/math-academy-id "e-8879" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=x^2$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right].$ What is $\\mathbf{r}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in[a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x) = x^2 \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in \\left[0,1\\right].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: x^2 \\cos\\theta, \\: x^2\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad\nx \\in \\left[0, 1\\right],\n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "6ab25451-ad5c-5437-b4ba-046b0bd30fe6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8880" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\cos{y}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y \\in [0, \\pi].$ What is $\\mathbf{r}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y) = 1-\\cos{y} \\geq 0$ for all $y \\in [0, \\pi].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta) = \n\\big\\langle (1-\\cos{y}) \\cos\\theta, \\: \ny, \\: \n(1-\\cos{y}) \\sin\\theta \n\\big\\rangle,\n\\quad\ny \\in[0, \\pi],\n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdaf06ad-8a5e-55d8-857e-f7335543f3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168188} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a8a98-3f2a-5ac2-b54b-cdb02e7eb5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168189} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f4ca0c-8d40-5d35-9bc7-f5dd04d80227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168190} {:answer/id #uuid "02783d3a-d9a1-5452-822d-966d5e0130ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168191} {:answer/id #uuid "6841d782-b072-579a-b093-77118b70440e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdaf06ad-8a5e-55d8-857e-f7335543f3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168188} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c16368-7829-5fe0-a98e-4f96d9855360" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bba1677a-6311-54bf-bad0-6a9e581cdd7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106058" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=1+{y}^{2}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=1+{y}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-1,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1+{y}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2y.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b50d89-d0f6-5689-bf70-b769dc21f3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373492} {:answer/id #uuid "90e4b619-bbc9-58da-ba47-c0bb4a3580e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373493} {:answer/id #uuid "795ae949-b89f-56fe-84ca-d7cc5a303cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373494} {:answer/id #uuid "ba638e4c-084b-5ed3-ae04-d9adfc24012b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373495} {:answer/id #uuid "986271dd-3153-5038-a58d-1697523056bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba638e4c-084b-5ed3-ae04-d9adfc24012b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373495} :answer-field/id #uuid "16c56699-6d0e-5eef-b9c7-5d6e8126124b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57494b1e-2aae-569c-8de2-ca632c8af9a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-109718" :question/problem "$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\text{_______},\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$ What is the second component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n  \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,4],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de3932b8-671d-59d3-9da3-72c605d48660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373499} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a7aa63-8ea7-5b4a-b540-8010c76d7b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-{x}^{2})\\operat