ion vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={y}^{3}-{y}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e75a2e9d-4fe7-5cbc-960b-fb8b487255bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {csc}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373527} {:answer/id #uuid "d020ed78-3866-5b40-b441-68cd15dd7a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373528} {:answer/id #uuid "41467bd7-8aa0-592e-8e52-6876b88ea14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {csc}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373529} {:answer/id #uuid "b09501e8-7726-56e9-b47c-736d73038450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373530} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6f1328-df11-5df1-9995-16b6bf9d5376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡y\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d020ed78-3866-5b40-b441-68cd15dd7a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorn