1-4963-5431-b77a-d640902c4765" :question/math-academy-id "q-161030" :question/problem "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1].$ What is the second component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$  \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca18160-0fb0-57a9-9c4d-476987dc1f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373555} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2fe6df-7e12-568b-94f1-c84066de6357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373556} {:answer/id #uuid "01d05f88-8eca-58a3-910a-dabd48c459d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373557} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb9389e-b30d-56f6-b83a-7363bcbb43a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373558} {:answer/id #uuid "baf6e666-ddaf-5805-99bd-e4dbd50564b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{z}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d05f88-8eca-58a3-910a-dabd48c459d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373557} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b7b9a3-8f4c-50b6-8dac-51ee01bd5fb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b19b14d7-b912-54d5-b08a-3fd983a437dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161036" :question/problem "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\text{__________},\\,({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={2}^{z}-1$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,2].$ What is the first component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)={2}^{z}-1\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,2].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [0,2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e0eccc0-4369-5250-b1e2-90b150cd772e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373562} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2c4670-3794-5753-94a3-9d6ffe783e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373563} {:answer/id #uuid "77f33888-294a-5a6d-b9db-e199c547cd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373564} {:answer/id #uuid "6089b59a-e049-54e2-aedc-1cc5e0b7995f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373565} {:answer/id #uuid "6a81dc70-d8d2-5235-909f-42fd500ed39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2c4670-3794-5753-94a3-9d6ffe783e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373563} :answer-field/id #uuid "f72cfa62-153a-5d29-905c-4b714d529eb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fd05543-77d3-5883-9430-aa64bbd0f377" :question/math-academy-id "q-161061" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi /4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb63ff44-9243-5879-9176-5e6cf970ac6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373569} {:answer/id #uuid "1fdd035d-b276-5cbf-b812-bccb5b6e2fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373570} {:answer/id #uuid "68b78e65-4151-5712-b5c0-b0dc3c601c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373571} {:answer/id #uuid "741877b6-0d83-5bb3-944f-8a02758d21d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373572} {:answer/id #uuid "a3957991-6dd9-5f30-a940-bd077d74358d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "741877b6-0d83-5bb3-944f-8a02758d21d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373572} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b4a7d6-5c4e-5232-84cc-995ba9d8a1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3db431ad-aaac-579c-961c-14884ec8b4d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161063" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $3$rd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ and    \n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince $f={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2z{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82a54cc3-ca46-5b66-b02e-cc4ec1cfee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin y\\cos^{3} y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169070} {:answer/id #uuid "c20c86d9-0959-5f44-9492-658b5eb50883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin 2y\\cos y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169071} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ebad9e-77c4-5c15-9810-77a468ff78e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin y\\cos^{2} y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169072} {:answer/id #uuid "ff879225-7b68-56ed-a06b-ae7dcda19dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin 2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169073} {:answer/id #uuid "2214456d-c3d9-5c9c-a6a5-b4cbab39d0dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin^{2} 2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82a54cc3-ca46-5b66-b02e-cc4ec1cfee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin y\\cos^{3} y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169070} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2295a86-f4f1-52eb-b137-a20b7ef4a93c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5660a1ea-1c96-5f39-8bde-a992c1f8c03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161069" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={cos}^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd  component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={cos}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f94326b-17f1-558b-b9b0-47872b935c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373576} {:answer/id #uuid "35d8aec9-08d4-552e-adab-16dc04f9f331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373577} {:answer/id #uuid "7a15cf79-b0b4-5bee-a738-38c97cb93daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373578} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce600dd-4a52-5663-87a2-88fa29d8d54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373579} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb990c6-bf6e-5223-98be-3c7a32b025c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a15cf79-b0b4-5bee-a738-38c97cb93daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373578} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a5d101-527a-5b26-95d6-1df523e911e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd6b23cf-0f4d-571e-a46d-8551c4a4d4b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161072" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={x}^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{2},$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2{x}^{3},\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e131b8aa-f2cc-51bb-b13f-63ad0cf88bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373583} {:answer/id #uuid "57c5b7ff-eaa7-5818-b02d-10141731da15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373584} {:answer/id #uuid "e255beb6-b10b-567e-b66f-8ed34e10b62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373585} {:answer/id #uuid "45c24d99-52d0-54c9-bf49-ab478c5b1f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373586} {:answer/id #uuid "2c65c1ca-6332-5a59-af35-eb03f0897829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e131b8aa-f2cc-51bb-b13f-63ad0cf88bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373583} :answer-field/id #uuid "584e7127-31ef-5e9f-92c8-46f1b37a4dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b3145af-3a0e-5c06-85fe-84edbe5ec50f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161073" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\text{_______}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [1,4]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [1,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eecceef7-8490-5582-8b96-d2526d8041c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373590} {:answer/id #uuid "06f0db32-985b-516b-b7ef-42e7e4e4380b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373591} {:answer/id #uuid "c0172939-9364-5913-aa3b-6f0b7d98dcfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373592} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f85f4d-2d12-5506-9264-d853cae57d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373593} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b4f2df-3b70-5b68-b392-34c9b7228b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06f0db32-985b-516b-b7ef-42e7e4e4380b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373591} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c761b0-acf0-50ae-a4eb-a81b658081d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3832d2fa-673b-5b53-98a7-cbe8b02e2624" :question/math-academy-id "q-161093" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae28bc2-f9a0-5d59-bace-6845445f5266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{3}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169076} {:answer/id #uuid "e84bd21c-42f3-570e-9500-c24f14ce38e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{3}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169077} {:answer/id #uuid "ea344160-f2e6-5a60-b449-05b530fdcad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169078} {:answer/id #uuid "00df6180-a1be-57fa-83f1-d9db68dffca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169079} {:answer/id #uuid "297c0a53-60c2-545d-a4e9-631696e3a55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea344160-f2e6-5a60-b449-05b530fdcad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169078} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1a3809-886e-5fc6-b6f6-9ef2ef43a75a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "929a0232-4773-59cb-9c62-800f13322539" :question/math-academy-id "q-161095" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={x}^{4}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-2,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=4{x}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8a889d6-1cf3-559e-96ba-49e862b79b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{2}\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373597} {:answer/id #uuid "633b3186-c1a4-570c-b30b-4d3ac8934d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373598} {:answer/id #uuid "e18392ea-8064-5398-bf6f-b4a481622fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373599} {:answer/id #uuid "ba67d8ba-fdbc-5d94-a6e8-fb880a165b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373600} {:answer/id #uuid "e1661cd3-48da-5584-b957-23a1fbb9030d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{z}^{3}\\sqrt{1+z\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba67d8ba-fdbc-5d94-a6e8-fb880a165b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373600} :answer-field/id #uuid "54935dcb-30ae-5216-8fbc-1bdd99135d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eb47bd6d-1b90-53a8-94d7-94b3a1b41796" :question/math-academy-id "q-161097" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=z\\sqrt{z}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=z\\sqrt{z}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86c1a04f-830b-5a1f-97b9-70453b78a08c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373604} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e7e052-255f-5bd3-8480-4790c13f9566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373605} {:answer/id #uuid "5d17dfea-8619-5088-b99e-44219f2a3d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373606} {:answer/id #uuid "2946b410-32b1-5d52-a4a8-6301339754e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373607} {:answer/id #uuid "5e934144-6c85-5e8c-bf19-73988ec05b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e934144-6c85-5e8c-bf19-73988ec05b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373608} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe77e0e4-0c2a-5bad-8b8a-fb682d469194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e85c59df-7be0-577e-b503-9d8d25c6e082" :question/math-academy-id "q-161099" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
